I read the drawing as a 9-membered dilactone core of the antimycin type, carrying an acylated 4-methoxypyridine-2-carboxamide.

**Pyridine part.** It has a 4-OMe and a 3-O-acyl group, and its 2-position carries the C(=O)NH amide. The acyl chain is drawn as a zigzag, and I counted it as a decanoyl (10 carbons including the C=O). This count is the least certain part of the reading.

**Ring.** Going round the 9-membered ring: C(=O), O, CH(CH3), CH(OC(=O)iPr), CH(CH2Ph), C(=O), O, CH2, CH(NH–). The ester on the ring is isobutyrate, and Ph is expanded to a phenyl group.

**Stereochemistry.** There are four stereocentres, each assigned from its wedge or hash:
- the NH-bearing carbon, with the N on a hashed bond;
- the CH(CH2Ph) carbon, with the benzyl on a wedge;
- the CH(O-isobutyryl) carbon, with the O on a hashed bond;
- the CH(CH3) carbon, with the methyl on a wedge.

I checked each @/@@ by comparing the neighbour order in the SMILES against the drawn geometry, with the implicit H on the opposite side from the wedged or hashed substituent.

SMILES: COc1ccnc(C(=O)N[C@H]2COC(=O)[C@H](Cc3ccccc3)[C@@H](OC(=O)C(C)C)[C@H](C)OC2=O)c1OC(=O)CCCCCCCCC

I read the structure in three parts and wrote the SMILES from that reading.

**Pyridine fragment.** The ring has OMe at C4. Position 3 carries an ester, OC(=O)CH2OPh. Position 2 carries C(=O)NH– attached to the ring system on the right.

**Nine-membered dilactone ring.** Going around the ring, the atoms are:
- the NH-bearing CH (the α-carbon);
- a C(=O) that is bonded through O to the next carbon;
- CH(CH3);
- CH bearing the isobutyrate, OC(=O)CH(CH3)2;
- CH bearing the benzyl group;
- a C(=O) bonded through O to a CH2, which closes back to the α-carbon.

**Stereochemistry.** I assigned each centre from the wedges and hashes and checked the CIP labels by hand:
- α-carbon, N on a hash: S.
- CH(CH3), methyl on a wedge: S.
- CH(O-isobutyrate), O on a hash: R.
- CH(CH2Ph), benzyl on a wedge: R.

I then converted each label to @/@@ for the SMILES atom order. I did not run the string through a parser.

SMILES: COc1ccnc(C(=O)N[C@H]2COC(=O)[C@H](Cc3ccccc3)[C@@H](OC(=O)C(C)C)[C@H](C)OC2=O)c1OC(=O)COc1ccccc1
